.TH TYPO 1
.SH NAME
typo \- find possible typographical errors
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B typo
[
.B -13nr
]
[
.B -m
.I max
]
[
.B -t
.I threshold
]
[
.I file ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Typo
reads the
.I files
(standard input if none) and prints each word in them that is not a common English word,
with its index of peculiarity, in decreasing order of that index,
words of the same index in alphabetical order.
An index of 10 or more is peculiar: such a word holds a sequence of letters
seen almost nowhere else in the document, and is often a misspelling.
Before them it prints each word that repeats the word before it,
as in
.LR "the the" ,
a common typographical error.
.PP
Each word is printed on a line with its address, its index in brackets, and the word,
.IP
.EX
/sys/man/1/rc:846:18 [15] ctrl
.EE
.PP
and each repeat with its address and the word, as it is in the text, followed by
.LR repeats .
The address is the file, the line and the column, counted in characters from 1,
of the repeat or of the word's first occurrence;
standard input has the name
.LR stdin .
Button 3 on the address in
.IR acme (1)
opens the file there, by the
.IR plumber (4)'s
rules for
.IB file : line : column\f1.
.PP
A word repeats the one before it when the two are the same, upper and lower case aside,
with only white space between them,
across lines and files.
For this a word is a run of characters other than white space,
less any characters that are neither letters nor digits at either end;
it may hold letters, apostrophes and hyphens, and a run with anything else in it,
such as a number, breaks the sequence, as does a
.IR troff
request, whose line is not read.
.PP
The statistics are the document's own.
.I Typo
counts each digram and trigram of letters in its words,
the start and end of a word counting as a letter, so that
.L once
gives the digrams
.LR "\ o" ,
.LR on ,
.LR nc ,
.L ce
and
.L "e\ "
and the trigrams
.LR "\ on" ,
.LR onc ,
.L nce
and
.LR "ce\ " .
For each trigram
.I xyz
of a word it takes
.IP
.EX
(log n(xy) + log n(yz))/2 \- log n(xyz)
.EE
.PP
the log of a count of 0 or 1 being \-10, and the word's index is the largest of these, at least 0.
The counts are approximate, as
.IR typo 's
always kept them: each is a small counter, incremented with a probability
that falls as it grows, so that its value is about the logarithm of the count.
They start from
.BR /sys/lib/typo/salt ,
the counts of a sample of English.
The words of
.B /sys/lib/typo/w2006
are not printed.
.PP
For the index a word is a run of two or more letters, any letter that Unicode counts as one,
and its upper case is mapped into lower case.
Lines that begin with
.L .
or
.L '
are
.IR troff (1)
requests and are ignored.
An apostrophe within a word is dropped,
.L %
is ignored, and a word hyphenated at the end of a line is joined to its rest.
.PP
The options are:
.TP
.B -1
Print the Sixth Edition's output: one column of index and word, with no addresses and no repeats.
.TP
.B -3
Print the Sixth Edition's output in three columns, by
.IR pr (1),
with the header
.BI "Possible typo's in" " file"\f1,
as it printed by default.
.TP
.B -n
Start the counts from nothing, not from English:
for a document in another language.
.TP
.B -r
Print no repeats.
.TP
.BI -m " max
Print at most
.I max
words.
.TP
.BI -t " threshold
Print only words whose index is at least
.IR threshold .
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
% typo /sys/man/1/cb | sed 3q
/sys/man/1/cb:20:1 from repeats
/sys/man/1/cb:45:23 [15] macros
/sys/man/1/cb:3:17 [14] beautifier
.EE
.SH FILES
.TF /sys/lib/typo/LICENSE.pike
.TP
.B /sys/lib/typo/salt
digram and trigram counts of English, as Research Unix kept them in
.BR /usr/lib/salt
.TP
.B /sys/lib/typo/w2006
common words, one a line
.TP
.B /sys/lib/typo/LICENSE
Caldera's license for
.B salt
and
.B w2006
.TP
.B /sys/lib/typo/LICENSE.pike
the license of Rob Pike's
.I typo
.SH SOURCE
plan9web's
.B src/cmd/typo.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR spell (1),
.IR deroff (1)
.br
Robert Morris and Lorinda L. Cherry,
``Computer Detection of Typographical Errors'',
.I IEEE Transactions on Professional Communication
PC-18, 1 (March, 1975), pp. 54-64.
.SH HISTORY
.I Typo
is Robert Morris and Lorinda Cherry's, of Bell Labs.
It first appeared in the Third Edition of Research Unix (1973),
and was much used until
.I spell
came in the Fifth.
This one follows the Sixth Edition's
.BR /usr/source/s2/typo.c ,
whose output it gives for a document in ASCII with
.B -1
or
.BR -3 ,
and its
.B salt
and
.B w2006
are that edition's
.BR /usr/lib/salt
and
.BR /usr/lib/w2006 ,
under Caldera's 2002 license for Research Unix through the Seventh Edition.
The
.B salt
is from Rob Pike's copy, in
.BR https://github.com/robpike/typo ,
with a CR taken out before each of its 113 newlines,
which a conversion to CRLF had put there.
.PP
The report of repeats, the addresses, one word a line, and
.BR -m ,
.B -t
and
.B -r
are from Pike's
.I typo
in Go (2023), whose
.B -n
is
.B -m
here, as
.B -n
was the Sixth Edition's.
His repeats pass over punctuation between the words, as in
.LR "null, null" ,
and over numbers and other runs with no letters,
which end a repeat here.
His index, the root mean square of the trigrams' indices over the document's counts alone,
is not taken.
With five misspellings planted in each of 52 documents
(8 manual pages through
.IR deroff (1),
4 of plan9web's documents and
.BR /lib/constitution ,
4 times each),
transpositions, dropped, doubled and changed letters of words used elsewhere in the document,
this
.I typo
ranks 46 of the 260 among its first 10 words, 113 among 25 and 176 among 50,
and Pike's 1, 24 and 64.
.PP
The paper says that the index adopted was the root mean square of the trigrams' indices;
the program took the largest, and so does this one.
.SH BUGS
Because of the mapping into lower case and the dropped apostrophes,
a word's index may be printed in a form not in the original text:
.L process's
is printed as
.LR processs ,
though its address finds it.
.PP
Requests within a line and
.IR troff 's
escapes are read as text.
